The regular pattern of the construction of the Biskupin hillfort is repeated in the case of other Lusatian settlements of the Early Iron Age in Poland as well (e.g. Sobiejuchy, Izdebno in Pałuki, Jankowo in Kuyavia). The reasons why the people of the Lusatian culture built fortified hillforts with regular internal row-built housing are the subject of an unfinished scholarly discussion. From an economic point of view it is not rational to live in the enclosed area of a hillfort surrounded by water or marshes. For that reason at least three attempts to explain this phenomenon have appeared in the literature on the subject: 1. the threat from nomadic peoples – the Cimmerians and the Scythians;
 2. intra-tribal battles;
 3. a willingness to imitate Greek cities, with which the communities of the Lusatian culture could have become acquainted thanks to long-distance trade with Greek cities and colonies.
The cause of the fall of Biskupin and of other hillforts of this type is similarly unknown. Archaeologists consider above all the following possibilities:
 * destruction of the settlements by the Scythians (some of the hillforts were indeed burnt down, and Scythian arrowheads were also found in some of them);
 * a rise in the water level of the lakes as a result of a deterioration of the climate;
 * changes in the natural environment around the hillfort and the necessity of moving to other areas. At present there is a shop in one "flat" in a longhouse selling good-quality replicas of prehistoric pottery. The choice was really wide, and the shop assistant in period clothing, who also makes the pottery herself, was truly delighted with us, because most of us could not resist and we bought beautiful vessels. We then moved on to the Slavic settlement, which is also very nicely reconstructed.
